console.log(new C().run());
```

`tsc --strict --target es2021 --outDir out c.ts && node out/c.js` prints `get,set 0`.

The emit writes unconditionally, with the short-circuited value:

```
__classPrivateFieldSet(this, _C_instances, __classPrivateFieldGet(this, _C_instances, "a", _C_f_get) && 1, "a", _C_f_set);
```

`??=` (getter returning 0, so not nullish) and `||=` (getter returning 1) behave the same way: the setter runs with the current value.

`get`, as with `--target es2022` or running the source directly. For `&&=` the spec returns `lval` without `PutValue` when it is falsy (https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-assignment-operators-runtime-semantics-evaluation); `||=` and `??=` are analogous. Public members are already lowered correctly (`o.p && (o.p = v)`).

Observable whenever the setter has side effects or there is no setter. With a private method the downlevel code throws `TypeError: Private method is not writable` even when no write should happen (`this.#m ||= 1`), though that case already gets TS2803.

The fix is probably in the logical-assignment branch for private identifiers in `transformers/classFields.ts`: emit `get && set(...)` (resp. `||`, and a nullish check for `??=`) instead of `set(get && v)`. Closest existing issues: #65287 (different defect, closed) and #39172 (public members, 2020).

