Downlevel emit of `||=`, `&&=`, `??=` and `**=` evaluates a member target twice when it has `!`, `as` or `satisfies`

> This issue was drafted with the help of LLMs (Claude and Codex); I reviewed it and verified the reproductions.

logical assignment evaluated twice, `||=` non-null assertion downlevel, `??=` type assertion side effect, `**=` `as` Math.pow evaluated twice, PartiallyEmittedExpression logical assignment, ES2020 target logical assignment side effects

- This is the behavior in every version I tried (4.0.2, 4.9.5, 5.9.3, 6.0.3, 7.0.2 and 7.1.0-dev.20261103.1), and I reviewed the FAQ for entries about type assertions and downlevel emit

```
let key = 0;
const obj: Record<number, number> = {};
obj[++key]! ||= 1;
console.log(key, JSON.stringify(obj));
```

Compiled with `tsc --target es2020 input.ts`, which reports no errors.

```
"use strict";
let key = 0;
const obj = {};
obj[++key] || (obj[++key] = 1);
console.log(key, JSON.stringify(obj));
```

`node input.js` prints `2 {"2":1}`: `++key` runs again in the assignment branch, and the value is written to a different property than the one that was read.

The same happens with `&&=` and `??=` whenever the assignment branch runs, with `as`, `satisfies` and angle-bracket assertions around the target, and with an effectful receiver: `getObject().x! ||= 1` calls `getObject()` twice. `**=` with `--target es2015` has the same problem: `obj[++key]! **= 2` is emitted as `obj[++key] = Math.pow(obj[++key], 2)`.

The `!` is type-only and should not change evaluation: `++key` should run once, and the output should print `1 {"1":1}`, as `node input.ts` does with Node's type stripping. Without the `!`, the same file is emitted as `obj[_a = ++key] || (obj[_a] = 1)`, and with `--target es2021` the operator is kept as is; both print `1 {"1":1}`.

Steps, in an empty directory with Node.js 26.13.0:

```
npm install --save-exact typescript@7.0.2
npx tsc --target es2020 input.ts
node input.ts
node input.js
```

The type eraser replaces `!`, `as`, `satisfies` and angle-bracket assertions with a `PartiallyEmittedExpression` ([typeeraser.go#L263-L267](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/f0accfdfe02cb199b0f6c8ccab68e3419f33862a/tsc/internal/transformers/tstransforms/typeeraser.go#L263-L267)). The logical assignment transform skips only parentheses before testing for an access expression ([logicalassignment.go#L37-L41](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/f0accfdfe02cb199b0f6c8ccab68e3419f33862a/tsc/internal/transformers/estransforms/logicalassignment.go#L37-L41)), so the test fails and the same `left` node becomes both the read and the write target ([L96-L107](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/f0accfdfe02cb199b0f6c8ccab68e3419f33862a/tsc/internal/transformers/estransforms/logicalassignment.go#L96-L107)). The exponentiation transform skips nothing before its element and property access tests and falls back to `target = left` and `value = left` ([exponentiation.go#L37-L69](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/f0accfdfe02cb199b0f6c8ccab68e3419f33862a/tsc/internal/transformers/estransforms/exponentiation.go#L37-L69)). In the 6.0.3 JavaScript build, skipping partially emitted expressions together with parentheses at these two places (`skipOuterExpressions(left, OuterExpressionKinds.Parentheses | OuterExpressionKinds.PartiallyEmittedExpressions)`) makes every case above evaluate the target once; I have not built the Go compiler with the same change.

#39172 fixed the same duplication for plain element access targets in #39174, and that case still works. #44639 was the same kind of problem in optional chaining: a type assertion around the callee made the call lose `this`.

