`erasableSyntaxOnly`, `as`, `satisfies`, exponentiation, unary expression, `await`

Reproduced with TypeScript `6.0.3` and `7.0.2`. The Playground uses `6.0.3` to demonstrate the missing diagnostic.

[TypeScript Playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/?target=99&erasableSyntaxOnly=true&ts=6.0.3#code/PTAEAEFMCcEMGdYCMA2kDKBPAdgF1gB4Dy2KmAXKLtAK6QBQkBADgPbS6gBmN2AxrgCWrbKGyQA5rFyQAFAUrYaAWyQwAlKADe9UHtDRIuGtFEBaAqARiVa6KABUD0ACYA3PQC+9Ri3acEHD5uXgFhUVgAd1hBGQATeUoABWhWZUF4SAAeJVUYAD5NHX0DIxMI6NjQS2tcu0dndy8gA)

```
// @erasableSyntaxOnly: true
export function negate(x: number) {
    return -x as number ** 2;
}

export async function awaited(x: Promise<number>) {
    return await x as number ** 2;
}
```

Both functions compile without diagnostics, including with `--erasableSyntaxOnly`. The native builds emit `(-x) ** 2` and `(await x) ** 2`. Replacing the type syntax with spaces leaves `-x ** 2` and `await x ** 2`, both of which Node.js rejects with a `SyntaxError`.

Replacing `as number` with `satisfies number` or using chained assertions reproduces the issue. Erasure also produces invalid JavaScript when it exposes an unparenthesized `+`, `~`, `!`, `typeof`, `void`, or `delete` expression to the left of `**`.

The parser already rejects related cases where erasing `as` or `satisfies` changes the grouping of a binary expression (#63527 and #63661). Reporting a parser diagnostic here would be consistent with those fixes. In these examples, erasure leaves an invalid left operand for `**`.

Even if these forms remain accepted by default, a diagnostic under `--erasableSyntaxOnly` would still catch the unsafe erasure.

Explicitly parenthesized forms such as `(-x) as number ** 2` and `(await x) as number ** 2` should still compile.

- The original parser fix handles `as` and `satisfies` when erasure changes how surrounding binary operators bind. [microsoft/TypeScript#63527](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/63527)
- The follow-up parser fix covers the right associativity of `**`, where erasure can change grouping even at equal precedence. [microsoft/TypeScript#63661](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/63661)
- Ryan explained why ordinary parsing accepts `await a as any ** 1` and confirmed that current emit preserves its grouping by adding parentheses. [Ryan’s reply on microsoft/TypeScript#41755](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/41755#issuecomment-5445500640)

