lib.dom.d.ts: Transformer is missing the `cancel` member that WHATWG Streams defines

`lib.dom.d.ts` declares `interface Transformer` without the `cancel` member, but
the WHATWG Streams standard includes it and Node implements it. Any
`new TransformStream({ ..., cancel() {} })` therefore fails to compile with
TS2353, even though the handler is valid and gets called when the readable side
is cancelled.

Reproduced on TypeScript 6.0.3 and 7.0.2.

WHATWG Streams, `Transformer` dictionary
(https://streams.spec.whatwg.org/#dictdef-transformer) defines six members:

```
start, transform, flush, cancel, readableType, writableType
```

`lib.dom.d.ts` declares five of the six. `cancel` is missing:

```
interface Transformer<I = any, O = any> {
    flush?: TransformerFlushCallback<O>;
    readableType?: undefined;
    start?: TransformerStartCallback<O>;
    transform?: TransformerTransformCallback<I, O>;
    writableType?: undefined;
}
```

```
const ts = new TransformStream<Uint8Array, Uint8Array>({
  transform(chunk, controller) { controller.enqueue(chunk); },
  cancel() { /* client disconnected */ },
});
export { ts };
```

```
$ tsc --noEmit --target es2022 --lib dom,es2022 probe.ts
probe.ts(3,3): error TS2353: Object literal may only specify known properties,
and 'cancel' does not exist in type
'Transformer<Uint8Array<ArrayBufferLike>, Uint8Array<ArrayBufferLike>>'.
```

Identical output on 6.0.3 and 7.0.2.

Node 24.18.0, `stream/web`:

```
const ts = new TransformStream({
  transform(c, ctrl) { ctrl.enqueue(c); },
  cancel(reason) { console.log("transformer.cancel INVOKED", reason); },
});
await ts.readable.cancel("client-disconnect");
// -> transformer.cancel INVOKED client-disconnect
```

So `cancel` is not a dead optional member. It is the only hook that fires when
the readable side is cancelled, which for an SSE proxy is exactly the
client-disconnect case. Handlers here typically clear heartbeat and progress
intervals; removing them to satisfy the checker leaks a timer per abandoned
stream.

Filing this after trying the obvious local patch. Inside a module:

```
declare global {
  interface Transformer<I = unknown, O = unknown> {
    cancel?: (reason?: unknown) => void | PromiseLike<void>;
  }
}
```

This does **not** merge. Interface declarations must agree on their type
parameters, and `lib.dom.d.ts` uses `` while the natural
patch uses `unknown`. With `skipLibCheck: true` the resulting TS2428 is
suppressed, so the merge silently fails and `cancel` stays absent. Matching
`any` makes it merge, but then the default type arguments on every
`TransformStream` in the codebase degrade to `any`, which trades one problem
for another.

That dead end is the reason this is filed as a lib gap rather than handled
locally.

Add the member to `Transformer` in `dom.generated.d.ts`, with a callback type
next to the other three:

```
interface Transformer<I = any, O = any> {
    flush?: TransformerFlushCallback<O>;
    readableType?: undefined;
    start?: TransformerStartCallback<O>;
    transform?: TransformerTransformCallback<I, O>;
    cancel?: TransformerCancelCallback;
    writableType?: undefined;
}

type TransformerCancelCallback = (reason?: any) => void | PromiseLike<void>;
```

`lib.webworker.d.ts` has the same interface and needs the same change.

- Checked 6.0.3 and 7.0.2; both reproduce.
- Searched the issue tracker for `Transformer cancel` and `lib.dom TransformStream`
  before filing; the results were unrelated, so this does not look like a
  duplicate.
- TypeScript version 7 ships lib files inside the platform binary, so the
  reproduction uses the CLI rather than reading `lib.dom.d.ts` from disk.

