tsc -p app --noEmit --traceResolution, excerpt.

======== Resolving module 'dual' from '/repro/lib/src/index.ts'. ========
Using compiler options of project reference redirect '/repro/lib/tsconfig.json'.
Explicitly specified module resolution kind: 'NodeNext'.
Resolving in CJS mode with conditions 'require', 'types', 'node'.
File '/repro/lib/src/package.json' does not exist.
...

{ "name": "lib", "version": "1.0.0", "type": "module" }

{
  "name": "dual",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "type": "module",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": { "types": "./index.d.ts", "default": "./index.js" },
      "require": { "types": "./index.d.cts", "default": "./index.cjs" }
    }
  }
}

`lib` is an ES module (`"type": "module"`). On its own, `tsc -p lib` resolves `dual` with the `import` condition, and so does Node at runtime: `lib/dist/index.js` loads `dual/index.js` and `box.format` is `"esm"`. When `app` (`moduleResolution: bundler`) reaches `lib` through a project reference, TypeScript uses `lib`'s compiler options for the redirect (`NodeNext`), but resolves `lib`'s imports in CJS mode with the `require` condition. `lib`'s emitted `box: import("dual").Box` then picks up `dual`'s CommonJS declarations.

The error goes away if any one ingredient is removed:

| Variant | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `app` references `lib`, bundler resolution (the demo) | `"cjs"`: wrong |
| No `references`; `paths` to `lib/src` (`app` compiles `lib` with its own options) | `"esm"` |
| No `references`; `paths` to the prebuilt `lib/dist`. Same `.d.ts` file. | `"esm"` |
| `app` uses `module`/`moduleResolution: nodenext` | `"esm"` |

So the declaration file and package layout are fine on their own, and the referenced project's options are applied. Only the implied module format of the referenced project's files is wrong. A guess is that the source file's implied format is computed with the referencing program's options. Under `bundler` it isn't computed, and NodeNext resolution then treats the missing format as CommonJS.

The behavior is the same in TypeScript 6.0.3 and 7.0.2.

Real-world impact: Tiptap 3 ships separate `.d.ts` and `.d.cts` declarations. A monorepo with a `nodenext` library and a `bundler` web app that references it ends up with two unrelated copies of `@tiptap/core`'s types. Values typed by the library aren't assignable to the app's `Extensions`, and the library's `declare module '@tiptap/core'` augmentations aren't visible from the app.

