- Regression from tsc 6.0.3, which erases the field.
- Reproduces in 7.0.2, 7.1.0-beta and 7.1.0-dev.20261026.1.

```
function dec(target: object, key: string) {
  Object.defineProperty(target, key, { get: () => 'from prototype', configurable: true });
}

abstract class Base {
  @dec abstract data: string;
}

class Sub extends Base {
  declare data: string;
[... 4 more lines]
```

```
{ "compilerOptions": { "target": "es2022", "experimentalDecorators": true } }
```

`Base` gets a `data;` field, so every instance has an own `data: undefined` that shadows whatever the decorator put on the prototype. Prints `true undefined`.

```
class Base {
    data;
}
__decorate([
    dec
], Base.prototype, "data", void 0);
```

Same as tsc 6.0.3: the field is erased and only the `__decorate` call remains. Prints `false from prototype`.

```
class Base {
}
__decorate([
    dec
], Base.prototype, "data", void 0);
```

The field appears only when all three hold: `experimentalDecorators`, a decorator on the `abstract` property, and define semantics (`useDefineForClassFields`, the default at `target` ES2022 and later). `emitDecoratorMetadata` makes no difference. An undecorated `abstract` property and a decorated `declare` property are both still erased.

The likely cause is in `typeeraser.go`. For a decorated `declare`/`abstract` property under `experimentalDecorators`, it keeps the node so that `LegacyDecoratorsTransformer` can emit the `__decorate` call. But it rebuilds the node with `VisitModifiers`, which elides `AbstractKeyword`. `visitPropertyDeclaration` in `legacydecorators.go` then finds neither `NodeFlagsAmbient` nor `ModifierFlagsAbstract` and emits a field. `declare` escapes this because the node keeps `NodeFlagsAmbient`. Strada's `visitPropertyDeclaration` in `ts.ts` handles the same case by appending a `declare` modifier to the preserved node (`concatenate(modifiers, factory.createModifiersFromModifierFlags(ModifierFlags.Ambient))`).

We hit this in TypeORM entities: an abstract base declares a decorated `abstract` column, each subclass redeclares it with `declare`, and every entity instance gains an own `data: undefined` it doesn't have under tsc 6.

Missing namespace qualifier in `import =` emit across merged namespaces

namespace merging, import equals, import alias, namespace qualification, ReferenceError

- This changed between versions 6.0.3 and 7.0 (`main` at `49e6983021`).

https://www.typescriptlang.org/play/?ts=6.0.3#code/HYQwtgpgzgDiDGEAEA5JBvAUEnSIA8YB7AJwBclRJYFkBZDbXZg48peAGxCiiQGEMAXyY4RIzFWhxEqRswCWYNhQCCSALxI6AOn4BuUZQgB3JKoAUASkMigA

```
namespace N {
    export namespace M {
        export class C {}
    }
}

namespace N {
    import A = M.C;
    new A();
}
```

TypeScript 7 emits `var A = M.C;` in the second `N` block without qualifying `M` with `N`:

```
"use strict";
var N;
(function (N) {
    let M;
    (function (M) {
        class C {
        }
        M.C = C;
    })(M = N.M || (N.M = {}));
})(N || (N = {}));
[... 4 more lines]
```

Running the emitted JavaScript throws `ReferenceError: M is not defined`, since `M` is local to the first `N` IIFE.

`M` should be qualified with `N` in the emitted initializer, matching TypeScript 6.0.3:

```
(function (N) {
    var A = N.M.C; // Correctly qualified with the outer namespace `N`
    new A();
})(N || (N = {}));
```

- Referencing `new M.C()` directly in the second `namespace N` block is correctly emitted as `new N.M.C()`. The missing qualifier only affects `import =` declarations (such as `import A = M.C` or `import B = M`).
- Related to [microsoft/typescript-go#961](https://github.com/microsoft/typescript-go/issues/961), which addressed namespace qualification for expression references across merged declarations.

