`parseConfigFile` reports the circularity, and then `createSnapshot` never returns. The process has to be killed:

```
$ node repro.mjs
parseConfigFile: [ 'TS18000: Circularity detected while resolving configuration: {0}' ]
^C
$ npx tsc -p proj
error TS18000: Circularity detected while resolving configuration: {0}
$ echo $?
2
```

`createSnapshot` returns, and the project's config file parsing diagnostics contain TS18000, as `parseConfigFile` and `tsc -p proj` already report.

**Impact:** one circular `extends` in a repository makes `createSnapshot` hang with no error, where `tsc -p` reports TS18000 and exits 2 at once, so an analyzer on the API stalls on that repository instead of reporting what is wrong with it. The same happens when the cycle runs through a base config shared with a project that was opened first.

[`acquireConfigForProject`](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/96fffa9f650a7e75bf1448cf641d97e563a086cd/tsc/internal/project/configfileregistrybuilder.go#L285-L302) parses the config inside `entry.ChangeIf`, which holds that config's entry lock. [`reloadIfNeeded`](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/96fffa9f650a7e75bf1448cf641d97e563a086cd/tsc/internal/project/configfileregistrybuilder.go#L159) then calls [`updateExtendingConfigs`](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/96fffa9f650a7e75bf1448cf641d97e563a086cd/tsc/internal/project/configfileregistrybuilder.go#L169-L176), which runs `LoadOrStore` on every path in the parsed config's `ExtendedSourceFiles`, and `LoadOrStore` [locks an entry it finds](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/96fffa9f650a7e75bf1448cf641d97e563a086cd/tsc/internal/project/dirty/syncmap.go#L253). `parseConfig` builds that list from each extended config [and that config's own list](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/96fffa9f650a7e75bf1448cf641d97e563a086cd/tsc/internal/tsoptions/tsconfigparsing.go#L920-L925), [with nothing removed](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/blob/96fffa9f650a7e75bf1448cf641d97e563a086cd/tsc/internal/tsoptions/tsconfigparsing.go#L1090-L1094). When the chain cycles back to the opened config, the opened config is in its own list, so the goroutine waits on the entry lock it already holds. `tsc -p` does not walk the list under a lock, which is why it returns. The language server goes through the same `acquireConfigForProject`, but I reproduced only the API.

The `{0}` left in the TS18000 text, on the CLI too, has a separate cause and is not what this report is about.

I met this while building deadset-ts, the TypeScript analyzer of [deadset](https://github.com/cplieger/deadset), on the TypeScript 7 API. A pull request with the fix and a test follows.

