Non-null discriminant narrowing can use a stale fact after a later assignment

non-null assertion, discriminant narrowing, stale narrowing, assignment evaluation order, switch case mutation, nullable union, optimized discriminant path

This reproduces on unpatched main (commit 879f9867ac, before PR #64257). PR #64257's early evaluation-order commits (starting at 0801a60931) already fix this specific example; it's included here to document the general failure mode this PR's design addresses.

```
type Value =
      | { type: "1" }
      | { type: "2" }
      | { type: "3" }
      | { type: "4" }
      | { type: "5" }
      | { type: "6" }
      | { type: "7" }
      | { type: "8" }
      | { type: "9" }
[... 25 more lines]
```

No error is reported for either property access.

The non-null discriminant access is evaluated before the assignment on the right-hand side or in the switch case expression. The assignment may replace the value with undefined, after which the comparison or case can still match.

The matching branch can therefore execute with the variable set to undefined, but discriminant narrowing removes the nullable constituent and permits the property access.

Both property accesses should report that the value is possibly undefined.

A non-null fact established while evaluating value!.type must not be applied after a subsequently evaluated operand or case expression has reassigned or deleted the same reference or one of its prefixes.

This does not require modeling arbitrary side effects from opaque function calls. The mutation is syntactically present in the same expression and is evaluated after the discriminant access but before entering the branch.

The issue is easiest to observe with a union large enough to use the optimized discriminant path. Smaller unions currently behave differently, which is the inconsistency reported in #62511.

PR #64257 addresses both the original consistency problem and the evaluation-order requirement needed to apply the new non-null fact safely. The same underlying issue also applies to directly evaluated inline functions, constructors, tagged templates, computed names, decorators, and switch case expressions when they visibly mutate the matched reference.

